/-!
# The pullback of the ideal sheaf of an effective Cartier divisor

Let `h : Y ⟶ X` be a morphism of schemes and let `D ⊆ X` be an effective Cartier divisor whose
pullback is defined, that is, whose scheme-theoretic inverse image `h^{-1}D` is again an
effective Cartier divisor ([Stacks, Tag 01WV], `Stacks.Divisors.IsPullbackDefined`). Then the
ideal sheaf of `D` pulls back to the ideal sheaf of `h^{-1}D`,

`h^* ℐ_D ≅ ℐ_{h^{-1}D}`,

compatibly with the two inclusions into the structure sheaf of `Y`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Divisors.pullbackIdealSheafComapMap`: the canonical morphism `h^* ℐ_D ⟶ ℐ_{h^{-1}D}`,
  defined for an arbitrary morphism of schemes and an arbitrary closed subscheme.
* `Stacks.Divisors.pullbackIdealSheafComapIso`: the identification `h^* ℐ_D ≅ ℐ_{h^{-1}D}`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Divisors.pullbackIdealSheafComapMap_comp_idealSheafι` and
  `Stacks.Divisors.pullbackIdealSheafComapIso_hom_comp_idealSheafι`: **the map and the
  isomorphism are compatible with the inclusions into `𝒪_Y`.** Composed with the inclusion
  `ℐ_{h^{-1}D} ⟶ 𝒪_Y` they are the canonical map `h^* ℐ_D ⟶ 𝒪_Y` of
  `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.pullbackIdealIncl`.
* `Stacks.Divisors.exists_pullbackIdealSection`: the chart-level surjectivity. On an affine open
  `V` of the source contained in the preimage of an affine open `U` of the target, every element
  of the ideal `(D.comap h).ideal V` is the image of a local section of `h^* ℐ_D`.
* `Stacks.Divisors.epi_pullbackIdealSheafComapMap`: the canonical morphism is an epimorphism,
  for an arbitrary morphism of schemes and an arbitrary closed subscheme.

## Implementation notes

**No flatness.** The hypothesis is `Stacks.Divisors.IsPullbackDefined h D`, which by definition
says that `D.comap h` is an effective Cartier divisor, and nothing else; flatness of `h` enters
only in `Stacks.Divisors.isPullbackDefined_of_flat`, which produces that hypothesis. The two
invertibility hypotheses are used *only* to turn the epimorphism into an isomorphism: the
morphism itself, and its compatibility with the inclusions, hold for every morphism of schemes
and every closed subscheme.

**The map, and why the compatibility is free.** The closed subscheme `h^{-1}D` sits in the
commutative square `(h^{-1}D ⊆ Y) → (D ⊆ X)` whose lower edge is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.subschemeMap`, so the map is
`Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheafMap` of that square, read on the underlying ringed spaces. It
is by construction the factorisation of `h^* ℐ_D ⟶ 𝒪_Y` through the kernel defining
`ℐ_{h^{-1}D}`, whence the compatibility with the inclusions is
`Stacks.ClosedImmersion.idealSheafMap_comp_ι`, and no separate argument is needed. This is the
reason for choosing the kernel factorisation rather than a stalkwise comparison.

**Surjectivity is the only computation.** A morphism of sheaves of modules is an epimorphism
exactly when it is locally surjective, and both source and target are invertible, so an
epimorphism between them is an isomorphism
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isIso_of_epi_of_isInvertible`). Local surjectivity is checked on the
affine charts supplied by `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_appLE`: the ideal of `D.comap h` on an
affine open `V ⊆ h^{-1}U` is generated by the image of the ideal of `D` on `U`, and the image of
a section of `ℐ_D` over `U` under the unit of `h^* ⊣ h_*` is a section of `h^* ℐ_D` over
`h^{-1}U` whose ambient section is the image of the original one
(`Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.idealSection_pullbackIdealIncl`). So the ambient sections of `h^* ℐ_D`
over `V` form an ideal containing those generators, hence all of `(D.comap h).ideal V`; the
bridge `Stacks.Divisors.mem_ideal_iff_exists_idealSheafSection` identifies that ideal with the
sections of `ℐ_{h^{-1}D}` over `V`, and the inclusion of an ideal sheaf being injective on
sections (`Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection_injective`) upgrades the agreement of ambient
sections to an equality of sections.

**Spellings.** The scalar action on sections of a sheaf of modules is written through
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.smulSectionMap`, because `c • σ` for `c : Γ(Y, V)` and `σ` a section of a
sheaf of modules does not elaborate: the two spellings of the sections of the structure sheaf do
not share instances. This is the discipline of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.IdealSheafSections`, whose
`Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection_smulSectionMap` is the model for
`Stacks.Divisors.pullbackIdealSection_smulSectionMap` below.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WR)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OO](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OO)
